    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "<|channel>thought\n<channel|>That's a good start, thanks. Could you elaborate a bit more on the risks involved with each? Specifically, I want to highlight what happens when things go wrong—like what the fallout looks like if a single instance fails in a vertical setup versus a horizontal one. I want to make sure they understand the stability aspect.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Vertical scaling carries the significant risk that a single point of failure could halt all services if that primary instance encounters hardware issues or crashes, as it lacks redundancy. Conversely, horizontal scaling distributes load across multiple nodes, meaning that if one server fails, the others continue operating seamlessly to maintain system stability. Highlighting this difference is crucial because it demonstrates how our horizontal approach minimizes downtime and protects user experience during unexpected technical disruptions.",
      "source": "target",
